      "writtenOn": "2019-12-05T21:01:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e What is this change being used for again?\n\nMainly prebuilt module SDKs. I\u0027ve got some thought about trying to build the APEXes this way too, but that\u0027s not a priority atm.\n\n\u003e There\u0027s a good chance that you\u0027d want a 32-bit armv8 and another similar x86 in this list too.\n\nYou mean two additional archConfigs? I should have the arches corresponding to the products in build_unbundled_mainline_module.sh, neither more nor less, shouldn\u0027t I?\n\n\u003e And how is it different from the internal vendor/google/build/build_unbundled_mainline_module.sh?\n\nThat one iterates over products doing one build for each, but here we want to combine all four architectures in one build, e.g. getting all four arches in conscrypt-module-sdk-current.zip. Also, we need this in AOSP.",
      "parentUuid": "5d6332ec_a6f6b5d9",
      "revId": "41ce667f7c881876af14467a84c1ec5000af5090",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a27230e2_8b4f7d6b",
        "filename": "android/arch.go",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 1587,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-12-20T00:13:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e There\u0027s a good chance that you\u0027d want a 32-bit armv8 and another similar x86 in this list too.\n\nIf I\u0027m not mistaken build_unbundled_mainline_module.sh uses the aosp_\u003carch\u003e builds, so I lunched aosp_arm64 and looked at a lib built in out/target/product/generic_arm64/obj_arm, and it\u0027s still built for arm, not arm64:\n\n$ readelf -h out/target/product/generic_arm64/obj_arm/SHARED_LIBRARIES/libdexfile_external_intermediates/libdexfile_external.so\n  Class:                             ELF32\n  Machine:                           ARM\n\nThat\u0027s exactly what I get when lunching aosp_arm too. So afaics there\u0027s no difference between the 32-bit ISAs in arm and arm64. Same goes for x86(_64).",
      "parentUuid": "2d951440_147508d3",
      "revId": "41ce667f7c881876af14467a84c1ec5000af5090",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
